The current list fails on n3 because of rule ordering. In the state where user 2 holds drive and user 1 is ready, rule 9 (`u2_use`) fires before rule 13 (`u1_request_comm`). Using the drive first lets the holder move towards releasing it, but the best move is to make user 1's shortcut request while drive is still held. The listed best actions for that state confirm this: `u1_request_comm` reaches 0.444, while `u2_use` gives only 0.417.

The fix is to move the shortcut request rule above the user-2 and user-1 use rules. Everything else stays as it was. This is a targeted reordering, not a re-derivation of the full optimal strategy, so I have not verified it against all three training instances. The n1 and n2 results may shift, so they need rechecking.

